2.—(1) Any term in this Order that is defined in the First Schedule to the Air Navigation (91 — General Operating Rules) Regulations 2018 (G.N. No. S 441/2018) has the meaning given to that term in that Schedule unless the term is otherwise defined in sub‑paragraph (2).
(2) In this Order —
“ammunition” has the meaning given by section 4(1) of the Guns, Explosives and Weapons Control Act 2021;
“Home Team Science and Technology Agency” or “HTX” means the Home Team Science and Technology Agency established under section 3 of the Home Team Science and Technology Agency Act 2019;
“HTX officer” means a person appointed or employed under section 30 of the Home Team Science and Technology Agency Act 2019;
“jet‑propelled manpack system” or “JMS” means the aircraft known as the Gravity Industries Manpack Jet Suit Model MK4;
“police officer” has the meaning given by section 2(1) of the Police Force Act 2004;
“Project KESTREL” means the joint trial between the HTX and the SPF known as “Project KESTREL” in relation to the JMS;
“Singapore Police Force” or “SPF” means the Singapore Police Force established under section 3 of the Police Force Act 2004;
“specified activity” has the meaning given by paragraph 3(1);
“specified area” means any area set out in the First Schedule;
“specified period” means the period between 21 September 2026 and 20 September 2027 (both dates inclusive).

3.—(1) Subject to paragraph 4, a person mentioned in sub‑paragraph (2) is exempt from the provisions mentioned in sub‑paragraph (3) in respect of the conduct of any of the following activities under Project KESTREL (each called in this Order a specified activity), in any specified area:
(a)piloting or operating a JMS;
(b)carrying out any aerial work operation using a JMS;
(c)assisting in the activity mentioned in sub‑paragraph (a) or (b).
(2) A person mentioned in sub‑paragraph (1) is any of the following:
(a)the HTX in its capacity as an operator of a JMS under Project KESTREL;
(b)any person (including a police officer, an HTX officer, a contractor of the SPF or the HTX, or an employee of the contractor) who is authorised, employed or engaged by the HTX in its capacity as an operator of a JMS under Project KESTREL to conduct a specified activity.
(3) The provisions mentioned in sub‑paragraph (1) are —
(a)regulation 5(1) of the Air Navigation (47 — Aircraft Registration) Regulations 2026 (G.N. No. S 39/2026);
(b)regulation 30(2) of the Air Navigation (91 — General Operating Rules) Regulations 2018; and
(c)regulation 4 of the Air Navigation (137 — Aerial Work) Regulations 2018 (G.N. No. S 502/2018).

4. The exemption in paragraph 3 is subject to the following conditions:
(a)the safety conditions specified in the Second Schedule are complied with at all times during the flight or operation of the JMS for which the specified activity is conducted;
(b)there is in force, at all times when the specified activity is conducted, a policy of insurance with reasonably adequate coverage, maintained by or on behalf of the HTX insuring the HTX against any liability in respect of —
(i)the death of, or personal injury sustained by, any person; and
(ii)any property damage suffered by any person,
arising out of or in relation to the specified activity;
(c)the risk under the policy of insurance mentioned in sub‑paragraph (b) is assumed by an insurer who, at the time the policy is issued, is lawfully carrying on an insurance business in Singapore;
(d)the HTX must —
(i)keep full and proper records of the following information relating to Project KESTREL during the specified period:
(A)every flight log of every JMS, including flight times, flight altitude, weather conditions and visibility conditions;
(B)every maintenance schedule and record in respect of every JMS;
(C)every training programme to pilot or operate a JMS;
(D)a list of every individual who may pilot or operate a JMS;
(E)every test result for every JMS, including each anomaly;
(ii)provide the information mentioned in sub‑paragraph (i) for every calendar month during the specified period to the Authority on the first working day of the next calendar month; and
(iii)maintain the records mentioned in sub‑paragraph (i) for one year after 20 September 2027;
(e)on the occurrence of a reportable safety matter specified in the Third Schedule in the course of the conduct of a specified activity, the HTX —
(i)must immediately cease, or cause to be ceased, all activities under Project KESTREL;
(ii)must notify the Authority in writing of the occurrence as soon as practicable, but not later than 24 hours after the occurrence;
(iii)must submit a written report of the circumstances surrounding the occurrence to the Authority within 3 working days after the occurrence; and
(iv)must not resume or recommence the conduct of the specified activity, except with the Authority’s approval in writing;
(f)if the HTX flies or operates on a particular day any JMS in a specified area, the whole or a part of which is in a control zone, the HTX must —
(i)before the first flight or operation of a JMS on that day, obtain the consent of the Republic of Singapore Air Force not less than 15 minutes before the flight or operation; and
(ii)after the last flight or operation of a JMS on that day, inform the Republic of Singapore Air Force of the completion of the flight or operation.

SECOND SCHEDULE
Paragraph 4(a)
Safety conditions
1. The HTX must have in place adequate measures to ensure all of the following in respect of the conduct of any specified activity:
(a)the safety of every individual who conducts the specified activity;
(b)the part of the specified area in which the specified activity is conducted is not accessible to any member of the public;
(c)the specified activity is carried out in accordance with the applicable safety procedures specified by the HTX, and in a manner to avoid causing the death of or personal injury to any person or any property damage;
(d)every JMS that is used in the conduct of the specified activity is maintained in accordance with the requirements determined by the manufacturer of that JMS;
(e)every individual who conducts the specified activity is trained and competent to carry out that specified activity;
(f)no more than one JMS is in flight or operation at any time.
2. If the specified activity is the piloting or operating of a JMS, the HTX must also ensure that —
(a)the individual who pilots or operates the JMS —
(i)must not carry any ammunition when piloting or operating the JMS; and
(ii)must, at all times, keep a look-out for other aircraft or objects in the air;
(b)the JMS must not be flown or operated in the rain;
(c)the JMS must not be flown or operated at night, unless —
(i)the take‑off and landing areas, and the flight path, are sufficiently illuminated for this purpose; and
(ii)there is no obstacle in the flight path of the JMS;
(d)the JMS must not be flown or operated at an altitude higher than 60 metres above mean sea level;
(e)the JMS must not be flown or operated over any congested area of a city, town or settlement;
(f)the JMS must not be flown or operated over St John’s Island or Sisters’ Islands;
(g)the JMS, when in flight or operation, must maintain a horizontal distance of at least 10 metres from both of the following:
(i)any individual, other than the individual who pilots or operates the JMS, or any other individual who assists in the piloting or operating of the JMS;
(ii)any object, other than an object that is necessary for the operation of the JMS; and
(h)the JMS does not exceed a speed of 55 km/h.
3. To avoid doubt, paragraph 2(f) does not prevent a JMS from taking off from, or landing on, the shore of St John’s Island or Sisters’ Islands, as the case may be.
